# $vault_name — vault schema

This is a crate-wiki vault. Scope: **$scope**.

It exists because my recall of my own work is about three days long. Your job is to make what I
learn and what I've done accumulate instead of scatter. You're not a chatbot here — you're the one
who keeps this wiki true.

Read this file before touching anything.

## The layers, and the boundary that matters

| Layer | Path | Author | You |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 · Raw sources | `raw/` | the capture hook, me | **read, never write** |
| 2 · The wiki | `wiki/`, `index.md`, `log.md` | **you** | read + write |
| 3 · This schema | `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md` | me | read, never write |

**`raw/` is immutable.** It's ground truth, and never writing it is what makes a bad synthesis
recoverable — I can always re-read the source and have you redo the page. Editing raw to match the
wiki destroys the only copy of what actually happened.

**You don't edit this file.** If the schema is wrong, say so and I'll change it.

## Page types

Every page under `wiki/` is exactly one type. Something that fits none of them doesn't have a home
yet — tell me rather than inventing a sixth type.

| Type | Path | Holds | One per |
|---|---|---|---|
| `source` | `wiki/sources/` | what one raw source said, and why it mattered | raw file |
| `entity` | `wiki/entities/` | a person, project, repo, system, company | thing |
| `concept` | `wiki/concepts/` | an idea, pattern, technique | idea |
| `synthesis` | `wiki/syntheses/` | an answer worth keeping | question |
| `daily` | `wiki/daily/` | what happened on one day | day |

Skeletons carrying the exact frontmatter for each live in `.crate/templates/`. Don't copy them by
hand and don't improvise the shape — `crate new <type> "<Title>"` builds the page from them with the
dates and the title already filled in.

Every page carries a `summary:` — one line, and it's what `index.md` shows. Write it as you write
the page; a page without one shows up in the index as missing.

## Every page

**The filename is the title, and the H1 matches it.** `wiki/concepts/Session Parser.md` opens with
`# Session Parser`. That's what makes `[[Session Parser]]` resolve with no aliases and no ceremony.

**Prose, not bullets of bullets.** You're writing for someone who has forgotten everything — that's
the whole premise. Bullets are for lists that are genuinely lists.

**One line per paragraph.** Don't hard-wrap prose at a column — Obsidian renders a single newline
inside a paragraph as a line break, so wrapped text reads as shredded in Reading View. `crate fmt`
fixes this, so it's not something to get right by hand.

## Wikilinks are the mechanism

Link inline, in the sentence where the thing comes up — `the [[Session Parser]] drops tool output`,
not a "Related" heading at the bottom.

**Fan out generously.** A single ingested source usually touches 10–15 existing pages. That fan-out
*is* the compounding: a page nothing links to is invisible, and a page that links nowhere is a dead
end.

**Never link a page you haven't created.** A dead wikilink is a lie about what the wiki knows. If
something deserves a page and doesn't have one, create it or tell me.

## index.md and log.md

Both are generated. **Don't hand-write either one** — `crate` does, exactly, and your edits to
`index.md` are discarded the next time it's regenerated.

`index.md` catalogs every page exactly once, grouped by type, as `[[Page Name]] — one line`. That
one line comes from the page's own `summary:` frontmatter, so the way you write an index entry is to
write `summary:` on the page and run `crate index`. A page that isn't indexed is lost, whatever else
you did right — which is why the index is derived rather than remembered.

`log.md` is append-only: one entry per operation, `## [2026-07-17] ingest | Title`, written by
`crate log`. Never rewrite or reorder a line.

## Judgment — the part that isn't a script

**Extend before you create.** A new fact about an existing thing belongs on that thing's page. New
pages are for new things, not new mentions.

**Flag contradictions; never silently resolve them.** If a source disagrees with a page, show me
both and ask. This wiki is worth having only while it's true, and quiet overwriting is exactly how
it stops being.

**Cite, don't invent.** Every claim traces back to something in `raw/`. If you're inferring, say
you're inferring.

**Say when you don't know.** A gap named in `index.md` is worth more than a page that reads well
and is wrong.
